AIMED.سجّل({"slug":"takayasu-arteritis","عنوان":"Takayasu Arteritis","تمهيد":"<div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Sources retrieved (Rule B1) and source-chain warning</p><p><strong>[PK]</strong> none — no uploaded files in this project, so <strong>every claim here is [outside project knowledge]</strong>. <strong>[WEB]</strong> 2025 EULAR recommendations (secondary summary <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a> plus published abstract <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a></a>), 2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></a>, independent validation <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></a>, mortality-prediction analysis <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-5\" data-مصادر=\"5\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-5\" data-مصادر=\"5\">[5]</a></a>, ACR/VF 2021 guideline summary <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></a> and source PDF <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7\">[7]</a></a>, Asia-Pacific epidemiology <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a></a>, Chinese TAK guideline 2023 <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\">[9]</a></a>, 2025 meta-analysis of angiographic types <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a></a>, Curr Rheumatol Rep 2026 update (reference list only) <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a></a>, disease-activity tool <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></a>, outcome-measures review <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a>, case-report series <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\">[14]</a></a>, Vasculitis Foundation guideline page <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\">[15]</a></a>. Remainder <strong>[GEN]</strong>.</p><p><strong>Warning:</strong> the glucocorticoid doses and tapering targets in <em>Treatment</em> come from a secondary conference summary <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>, not the primary published document, and that summary&#39;s author discloses AI assistance. Treat those numbers as provisional and verify against the primary <em>Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases</em> text before prescribing.</p></div>","أقسام":[{"معرّف":"first-definition-and-overview","عنوان":"First: Definition and overview","html":"<div class=\"كتلة آلية\"><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Definition</span><span>chronic, idiopathic, granulomatous large-vessel vasculitis principally involving the aorta, its major branches, and — importantly for internal medicine — the pulmonary and coronary arteries, producing stenosis, occlusion, dilatation, or aneurysm. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Nosology</span><span>classified as large-vessel vasculitis (LVV) under the 2012 Revised International Chapel Hill Consensus Conference nomenclature, alongside giant cell arteritis. These are the only two primary LVVs. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Eponym</span><span>Mikito Takayasu, Japanese ophthalmologist, 1908 — reported wreath-like peripapillary arteriovenous anastomoses in a young woman. The disease was first described through its retinal consequence, not its arterial cause.</span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Synonyms</span><span>pulseless disease · aortic arch syndrome · occlusive thromboaortopathy · Martorell syndrome (historical, abandoned).</span></div></div><p><strong>Distinction from GCA</strong> rests on three axes: age at onset (TAK typically ≤40, and age ≤60 is an absolute requirement for classification <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></a>; GCA effectively never below 50), vessel distribution (TAK favours aorta and primary branches including subclavian, carotid, renal, and pulmonary; GCA favours cranial branches of the external carotid, though large-vessel GCA overlaps substantially), and geography/ethnicity. The overlap between late-onset TAK and large-vessel GCA is genuine and unresolved. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p>"},{"معرّف":"second-epidemiological-snapshot","عنوان":"Second: Epidemiological snapshot","html":"<p><strong>Clinical significance</strong> — a rare disease with disproportionate impact: it strikes young women in their productive years, causes irreversible arterial damage that accrues even during clinical remission, and its principal killers are cardiovascular. Increased mortality is largely driven by cardiovascular disease <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><div class=\"كتلة آلية\"><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Incidence</span><span>0.4–3.4 per million per year <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a></a>; also estimated at approximately 2.6 per million worldwide <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\">[9]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Prevalence</span><span>highest in Japan at around 40 per million, compared with about 9 per million in the USA <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Peak age</span><span>around 90% of patients are younger than 30 at onset <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\">[9]</a></a>. Second and third decades dominate. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Sex</span><span>strong female predominance. The magnitude varies geographically — no reliable pooled sex ratio located; do not quote a figure. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Geography</span><span>highest in Japan, India, Korea, and Thailand <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-8\" data-مصادر=\"8\">[8]</a></a>; also over-represented in Chinese, Turkish, Mexican, and other Latin American populations. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></span></div><div class=\"آلية-بند\"><span class=\"آلية-تسمية\">Mortality rate</span><span>no reliable pooled figure located. Reported survival varies widely across single-centre cohorts. Do not quote a survival percentage. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button></span></div></div><div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">A neglected disease, in a specific sense</p><p>Rarity plus a nonspecific inflammatory prodrome produces long diagnostic delay; the highest-burden populations are in low- and middle-income countries where advanced imaging and biologics are least available; and randomised trial evidence is almost absent. The systematic review informing the current EULAR recommendations concluded that high-quality, adequately sized randomised controlled trials in TAK are still lacking <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a></a>. Nearly all ACR/VF 2021 recommendations are <strong>conditional</strong> because the evidence quality is low <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\">[15]</a></a>. Carry that into any discussion of management.</p></div><h3 id=\"screening\">Screening</h3><div class=\"كتلة دليل\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">According to the 2025 EULAR recommendations</p><p><strong>There is no population screening programme for TAK, and none is recommended.</strong> At a prevalence of a few tens per million, no screening test could achieve acceptable positive predictive value. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><p>What the guidelines <em>do</em> mandate is screening <strong>within</strong> the diagnosed population: those with large-vessel vasculitis should be screened for cardiovascular comorbidities, with prophylactic and lifestyle interventions to mitigate risk <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. Also mandated: imaging should be used routinely to assess both disease activity and damage in all TAK patients <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a> — a stronger position than in GCA. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></p></div><h3 id=\"risk-factors-each-with-the-mechanistic-reason\">Risk factors, each with the mechanistic reason</h3><ul><li><strong>Female sex</strong> — the strongest demographic association. Mechanism unresolved; sex-hormone modulation of Th1/Th17 differentiation is hypothesised but not established. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li><li><strong>East, South, or Southeast Asian, Turkish, or Latin American ancestry</strong> — reflects underlying allele frequency differences, principally at HLA, rather than an environmental effect. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li>**HLA-B\\*52:01<strong> — the most consistently replicated genetic association across populations. HLA-B is a class I molecule presenting peptide to </strong>CD8+ cytotoxic T cells**; a particular B\\*52:01 binding groove is presumed to present a vascular or cross-reactive antigen, driving cytotoxic attack on the arterial media. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> for the association, <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button> for the mechanism.</li><li><strong>Other loci</strong> — <em>IL12B</em>, <em>FCGR2A/FCGR3A</em>, <em>IL6</em>, <em>MLX</em>, <em>RPS9/LILRB3</em>. Mechanisms map onto the Th1 axis (IL-12 → IFN-γ), Fc receptor signalling, and IL-6 amplification — the biological rationale for tocilizumab. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li><li><strong>Family history</strong> — first-degree aggregation reported but concordance low, consistent with modest polygenic risk plus an unidentified environmental trigger. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li><li><strong>Prior mycobacterial exposure</strong> — molecular mimicry between mycobacterial HSP65 and human HSP60 on stressed aortic endothelium, plus geographic co-distribution with tuberculosis. <strong>Unproven, and confounded by the fact that tuberculous aortitis is a diagnostic mimic rather than necessarily a cause.</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li><li><strong>No established modifiable risk factor.</strong> Smoking, hypertension, and dyslipidaemia do not cause TAK but compound its vascular damage — which is why they become targets once the diagnosis is made. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li></ul>"},{"معرّف":"third-pattern-recognition-the-archetypal-case","عنوان":"Third: Pattern recognition — the archetypal case","html":"<div class=\"كتلة مفتاح\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">The vignette to store</p><p>A <strong>24-year-old woman of South Asian ancestry</strong> presents after nine months of intermittent low-grade fever, fatigue, night sweats, arthralgia, and 6 kg unintentional weight loss. She has been investigated twice for pyrexia of unknown origin and treated presumptively for a viral illness and then for possible tuberculosis. Over the last two months her <strong>left arm tires and aches when she hangs washing</strong>, and she becomes lightheaded on turning her head.</p><p>On examination she is thin and unwell. The <strong>left radial and brachial pulses are absent</strong>; the right radial is normal. Blood pressure is <strong>unrecordable in the left arm and 168/98 mmHg in the right</strong>. There is a <strong>bruit over the left supraclavicular fossa and over the left carotid</strong>, and the left carotid is tender. A soft early diastolic murmur is audible at the left sternal edge. Lower limb pulses are present. Fundoscopy is normal.</p><p>Investigations: <strong>ESR 82 mm/h, CRP 48 mg/L</strong>, haemoglobin 96 g/L normochromic normocytic, platelets 480 ×10⁹/L, albumin 29 g/L, creatinine normal, urinalysis bland. Blood cultures sterile; IGRA negative; syphilis serology negative. <strong>CT angiography</strong> shows long-segment, smooth, <strong>concentric mural thickening</strong> with luminal narrowing of the left subclavian and left common carotid arteries, circumferential thickening of the aortic arch wall, and mild aortic root dilatation.</p></div><h3 id=\"discriminating-features\">Discriminating features</h3><ul><li><strong>Age and sex versus atherosclerosis:</strong> a woman under 40 with no metabolic risk factors.</li><li><strong>Lesion morphology:</strong> long, smooth, tapering, <strong>concentric</strong> stenoses with homogeneous circumferential wall thickening — versus short, eccentric, calcified, ostial plaques.</li><li><strong>Wall involvement precedes lumen involvement</strong> — invisible on luminal-only imaging.</li><li><strong>Involvement of the aorta itself</strong>, not merely its branches. Atherosclerosis and fibromuscular dysplasia do not thicken the aortic wall circumferentially.</li><li><strong>Blood pressure discrepancy between limbs</strong> with an ipsilateral bruit.</li><li><strong>Carotidynia</strong> — tenderness of the carotid artery. Rare but strongly suggestive, and one of only four variables in a validated activity model <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Raised acute-phase reactants</strong> in a young person with arterial stenosis.</li><li><strong>Versus fibromuscular dysplasia:</strong> FMD gives a beaded &quot;string of beads&quot; in the mid-to-distal renal or carotid artery, with normal inflammatory markers and no aortic wall thickening.</li><li><strong>Versus Buerger&#39;s disease:</strong> distal, small- and medium-vessel, heavy smoker, corkscrew collaterals, aorta spared. EULAR names Buerger&#39;s and FMD as the specific differentials to revisit when TAK appears refractory <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></li></ul><h3 id=\"pathognomonic-features\">Pathognomonic features</h3><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">None exist — state this plainly</p><p>No symptom, sign, laboratory value, or imaging appearance is pathognomonic for TAK. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><p>The nearest approach to specificity is the <strong>combination</strong> of long-segment concentric aortic and primary-branch wall thickening in a patient aged ≤40 with raised acute-phase reactants and no alternative explanation — highly suggestive, not diagnostic.</p><p><strong>Takayasu retinopathy</strong> (wreath-like peripapillary arteriovenous anastomoses) is highly characteristic but uncommon and mimicked by other causes of chronic ocular hypoperfusion.</p><p>The classification criteria are explicitly <strong>not</strong> diagnostic criteria. EULAR states as an overarching principle that classification criteria should not substitute for clinical diagnosis <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></p></div>"},{"معرّف":"fourth-pathogenesis-and-pathophysiology","عنوان":"Fourth: Pathogenesis and pathophysiology","html":"<div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Template subsections not applicable (Rule B4)</p><p><strong>Infectious organism, incubation period, infectivity, transmission:</strong> not applicable. TAK is not infectious and not transmissible. A mycobacterial molecular-mimicry hypothesis exists but is unproven. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p><p><strong>Histological subtypes with percentages, and staging system:</strong> not applicable. TAK is not neoplastic and has no TNM, FIGO, or Ann Arbor equivalent. Its anatomical classification is angiographic (below), which describes distribution, not stage.</p></div><h3 id=\"mechanistic-sequence-from-insult-to-manifestation\">Mechanistic sequence from insult to manifestation</h3><ol><li><strong>Unknown trigger in a genetically susceptible host.</strong> HLA-B\\*52:01-restricted antigen presentation is presumed central; the antigen is unidentified. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li><li><strong>Adventitia-first entry.</strong> Immune cells enter the arterial wall through the <strong>vasa vasorum</strong>, which supply the adventitia and outer media of large elastic arteries. This &quot;outside-in&quot; route is the single most useful mechanistic fact in the disease, because it explains why the adventitia and outer media are involved earliest, why <strong>wall thickening precedes luminal narrowing</strong> so imaging must interrogate the wall, and why the aorta and its large branches are targeted while small arteries without vasa vasorum are spared. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>Vascular dendritic cell activation</strong> in the adventitia recruits and activates T cells.</li><li><strong>CD4+ Th1 polarisation</strong> — IL-12-driven, producing IFN-γ, which activates macrophages, drives their fusion into <strong>multinucleated giant cells</strong>, and forms <strong>granulomas</strong> in the media. The histological signature. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>CD4+ Th17 polarisation</strong> — IL-17 production, recruiting neutrophils; less steroid-responsive than the Th1 axis, part of the explanation for glucocorticoid-refractory disease. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li><li><strong>CD8+ cytotoxic and γδ T cells</strong> release perforin and granzyme B onto medial smooth muscle cells → medial smooth muscle death. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>IL-6 and TNF-α amplification</strong> — drives the systemic acute-phase response (fever, ESR/CRP rise, anaemia of inflammation, thrombocytosis) and sustains local inflammation. Direct biological rationale for <strong>tocilizumab</strong> and <strong>TNF inhibitors</strong>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>Matrix metalloproteinase-mediated destruction</strong> — MMP-2 and MMP-9 degrade elastin and collagen. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li><li><strong>Two divergent structural outcomes</strong>, and which occurs determines the entire clinical picture:<ul><li><strong>Fibrostenotic</strong> (majority): intimal hyperplasia plus medial and adventitial fibrosis → concentric, long-segment <strong>stenosis then occlusion</strong>.</li><li><strong>Aneurysmal</strong> (minority): loss of medial elastic support → <strong>dilatation, aneurysm, dissection</strong>, including aortic root dilatation.</li><li>Both can coexist in the same patient — stenosis in one segment, aneurysm in another. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li></ul></li></ol><h3 id=\"downstream-pathophysiology-by-territory\">Downstream pathophysiology by territory</h3><ul><li><strong>Renal artery stenosis or mid-aortic narrowing</strong> → reduced renal perfusion pressure → <strong>RAAS activation</strong> → renovascular hypertension. The commonest mechanism of hypertension in TAK, often severe and early. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>Cerebral hypoperfusion</strong> from carotid and vertebral stenosis → dizziness, visual disturbance, syncope, TIA, stroke. Extensive collateralisation means symptoms lag far behind anatomical severity.</li><li><strong>Subclavian steal</strong> — stenosis proximal to the vertebral origin; arm exercise lowers distal subclavian pressure, reversing vertebral flow away from the brainstem.</li><li><strong>Coronary ostial stenosis</strong> → angina and myocardial infarction in a young woman with no atherosclerotic risk factors. TAK belongs on the differential for MI under 40.</li><li><strong>Pulmonary artery involvement</strong> → pulmonary hypertension and, in advanced disease, right heart failure. Frequently missed because it is not looked for.</li><li><strong>Aortic root dilatation</strong> → aortic regurgitation → LV volume overload → heart failure.</li><li><strong>Heart failure in TAK is multifactorial:</strong> hypertensive, valvular (AR), ischaemic (coronary ostial), pulmonary hypertensive, and occasionally directly myocarditic.</li><li><strong>Mesenteric and coeliac stenosis</strong> → post-prandial pain and weight loss; extensive collaterals make critical mesenteric ischaemia uncommon.</li><li><strong>Chronic kidney disease</strong> — from renal artery stenosis, hypertensive nephrosclerosis, occasionally an associated glomerulopathy.</li></ul><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">The measurement trap, which follows directly from the pathophysiology</p><p>If the subclavian arteries are stenosed, the cuff pressure in the arm <strong>underestimates central aortic pressure</strong>. A patient with 110/70 in both arms may have severe central hypertension driving target-organ damage. Conversely, treating to the arm reading can produce dangerous central hypotension.</p><p><strong>Four-limb blood pressure, and where all four are affected, central pressure measurement, are not academic refinements.</strong></p></div><h3 id=\"histopathology\">Histopathology</h3><p>Granulomatous <strong>panarteritis</strong> — all three layers. Adventitial fibrosis and vasa vasorum inflammation; medial destruction with lymphocytic and macrophage infiltrate, multinucleated giant cells, and loss of elastic lamina; intimal fibrous hyperplasia. <strong>Skip lesions</strong> are characteristic — involvement is segmental, with normal wall between diseased segments. In the burnt-out phase only dense fibrosis remains, which is why late biopsy can be misleadingly bland. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><h3 id=\"anatomical-angiographic-classification-hatanumano-types\">Anatomical (angiographic) classification — Hata/Numano types</h3><div class=\"لفافة-جدول\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Segments involved</th><th>Pooled prevalence <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a></a></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>Branches of the aortic arch</td><td>23.44–23.94% <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></td></tr><tr><td>IIa</td><td>Ascending aorta, arch, and arch branches</td><td>6.37–6.76% <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></td></tr><tr><td>IIb</td><td>Ascending aorta, arch and branches, plus thoracic descending aorta</td><td>7.92–8.20% <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Thoracic descending aorta, abdominal aorta, and/or renal arteries</td><td>5.13–5.40% (least common) <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Abdominal aorta and/or renal arteries</td><td>not retrieved <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button></td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Features of IIb and IV — diffuse involvement</td><td>43.49% (most common) <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Coronary involvement is designated <strong>C+</strong>, pulmonary artery involvement <strong>P+</strong> <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a></a>.</p><div class=\"كتلة جودة\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Interpretation caveat that matters more than the numbers</p><p>I² of approximately 78–83% means these pooled estimates conceal very large between-study variation, driven by geography and imaging modality. <strong>Do not use them as expected frequencies for your own population.</strong> Type V showed the greatest clinical severity and types IIb and III the lowest <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a></a> — meta-regression on observational data, not a validated prognostic tool. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p><p>Regional pattern: in Indian patients vasculitis generally began in the abdominal aorta involving the renal arteries and later spread to the thoracic aorta; in Japanese patients it generally began in the ascending aorta, arch, or its branches <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-10\" data-مصادر=\"10\">[10]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p></div>"},{"معرّف":"fifth-the-diagnostic-pathway","عنوان":"Fifth: The diagnostic pathway","html":"<h3 id=\"1-mode-of-presentation\">1. Mode of presentation</h3><p>Three routes, in descending order of clinical impression — <strong>relative frequencies could not be sourced</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button>:</p><ul><li><strong>With ischaemic or vascular complaints</strong> — limb claudication, dizziness, visual disturbance, chest pain, or incidental discovery of an absent pulse or inter-arm BP difference.</li><li><strong>With systemic inflammatory illness</strong> — fever, weight loss, arthralgia, malaise, raised ESR/CRP, often labelled pyrexia of unknown origin for months. TAK is a recognised cause of PUO in a young woman.</li><li><strong>Incidentally</strong> — asymmetric pulses at an unrelated examination; arterial wall thickening on CT or ultrasound done for another indication; increased aortic FDG uptake on PET for PUO or malignancy work-up. Incidental detection is increasing. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li></ul><div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">On the classical triphasic model</p><p>A systemic &quot;pre-pulseless&quot; inflammatory phase, then a vascular inflammatory phase, then a burnt-out fibrotic phase. A useful teaching scaffold but <strong>not reliably observed</strong>: many patients never have a recognised systemic phase, and inflammation and fibrosis coexist. <strong>Do not use phase assignment to make treatment decisions.</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p></div><h3 id=\"2-symptoms\">2. Symptoms</h3><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">No reliable frequency ranking located (Rule B4)</p><p>This subsection cannot be completed as the template requests, and padding it would be a rule violation. The only frequency dataset retrieved is a review of 43 published case reports in which fever was the most frequent symptom, followed by chest pain, claudication, and headache <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\">[14]</a></a>. Forty-three case reports, selected for publication precisely because they were instructive, cannot support a frequency ranking. <strong>I will not present those percentages as symptom frequencies.</strong> No pooled, population-based frequency data were located. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button></p></div><p><strong>Constitutional (inflammatory)</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — fever (IL-6 and TNF-α acting on the hypothalamic preoptic area via PGE₂; typically low-grade and prolonged) · fatigue and malaise (cytokine effects plus anaemia of inflammation plus, later, tissue hypoperfusion) · weight loss (inflammatory anorexia and catabolism) · arthralgia and myalgia (mechanism incompletely defined <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button>) · night sweats — <strong>overlapping entirely with tuberculosis and lymphoma, which is why those must be excluded before immunosuppression.</strong></p><p><strong>Upper limb ischaemic</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — arm claudication from subclavian, axillary, or brachial stenosis; often described as &quot;my arm gets tired,&quot; not as pain, and therefore not volunteered unless asked · cold, numb, or weak hand; frank gangrene uncommon because collaterals develop over months.</p><p><strong>Cerebrovascular</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — dizziness, lightheadedness, visual blurring (carotid or vertebral stenosis, worse on standing or head turning) · syncope · <strong>dizziness or drop attacks specifically on arm exertion</strong> (subclavian steal — this history is close to specific) · TIA or stroke, which <strong>may be the first manifestation of TAK in a young adult</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button> · headache (hypertensive, carotid inflammation, or cerebral hypoperfusion).</p><p><strong>Cardiac</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — chest pain must be mechanistically parsed, because three processes produce it:</p><ul><li><strong>Coronary ostial stenosis</strong> → exertional angina, or infarction.</li><li><strong>Aortitis of the ascending aorta or arch</strong> → deep, constant, sometimes interscapular pain from adventitial inflammation.</li><li><strong>Aortic dissection</strong> → abrupt, maximal-at-onset, migratory tearing pain. The emergency reading.</li><li>Note that <strong>angina carries +2 points</strong> in the 2022 ACR/EULAR criteria <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></a>, reflecting how strongly it associates with TAK in a young cohort.</li></ul><p>Also: <strong>exertional dyspnoea</strong> — from aortic regurgitation, hypertensive heart disease, pulmonary artery stenosis with pulmonary hypertension, or ischaemic LV dysfunction. In TAK, &quot;breathless young woman&quot; has a broad cardiac differential <em>within the same disease</em>. <strong>Palpitations</strong> — hypertensive LVH, atrial arrhythmia, or anaemia.</p><p><strong>Lower limb and abdominal</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — leg claudication (abdominal aortic or iliac stenosis; also mid-aortic narrowing in types III, IV, V) · post-prandial abdominal pain with food avoidance and weight loss (coeliac and mesenteric stenosis) · flank pain (renal infarction or renal artery involvement).</p><p><strong>Attributable to hypertension</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — headache, visual disturbance, epistaxis, or presentation with hypertensive emergency. In a hypertensive patient under 40, TAK belongs on the secondary-hypertension differential alongside renal artery FMD, phaeochromocytoma, primary aldosteronism, and coarctation.</p><p><strong>Ocular</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — transient visual obscuration or amaurosis (carotid or ophthalmic hypoperfusion) · chronic hypoperfusive (Takayasu) retinopathy → progressive visual loss · hypertensive retinopathy.</p><p><strong>Pregnancy-specific</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — worsening hypertension, pre-eclampsia, and fetal growth restriction from placental hypoperfusion with aortic or renal artery stenosis. Pregnancy in TAK is a specialist multidisciplinary problem.</p><h3 id=\"3-signs\">3. Signs</h3><p>Frequency ranking again not sourceable <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button> — but the <strong>2022 ACR/EULAR criteria weights give a defensible proxy for discriminating value</strong>, which is more useful than an unsourced list.</p><div class=\"كتلة دليل\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria — items and weights ^[3]</p><p>Female sex <strong>+1</strong> · angina <strong>+2</strong> · limb claudication <strong>+2</strong> · arterial bruit <strong>+2</strong> · reduced upper extremity pulse <strong>+2</strong> · reduced pulse or tenderness of a carotid artery <strong>+2</strong> · blood pressure difference between arms ≥20 mmHg <strong>+1</strong> · number of affected arterial territories <strong>+1 to +3</strong> · paired artery involvement <strong>+1</strong> · abdominal aorta plus renal or mesenteric involvement <strong>+3</strong>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></p></div><ul><li><strong>Reduced or absent peripheral pulse</strong> — most often radial or brachial, from proximal subclavian or axillary stenosis. Flow-limiting narrowing distal to which pulse pressure is damped. The origin of &quot;pulseless disease.&quot; <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>Inter-arm blood pressure difference</strong> — asymmetric subclavian stenosis. ≥20 mmHg is a criteria item <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></a>. <strong>Measure both arms in every young hypertensive patient.</strong></li><li><strong>Arterial bruit</strong> — turbulent flow across a stenosis. Auscultate systematically: both supraclavicular fossae, both carotids, the abdomen (epigastric and both renal areas), and both femorals. A bruit in a young person with no atherosclerotic risk factors is a high-value finding.</li><li><strong>Carotid tenderness (carotidynia)</strong> — adventitial and perivascular inflammation. One of four variables in a validated activity model, weighted 3 points <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></a>, so it carries information about <strong>activity</strong>, not merely presence.</li><li><strong>Hypertension</strong> — renovascular or mechanical.</li><li><strong>Early diastolic murmur at the left sternal edge</strong> — aortic regurgitation from root dilatation.</li><li><strong>Discrepant upper and lower limb blood pressure</strong> — mid-aortic or descending aortic narrowing; coarctation-like physiology.</li><li><strong>Absent or delayed femoral pulses with radio-femoral delay.</strong></li><li><strong>Signs of heart failure</strong> — raised JVP, crackles, displaced apex, peripheral oedema.</li><li><strong>Loud P2 and right ventricular signs</strong> — pulmonary artery involvement with pulmonary hypertension.</li><li><strong>Fundoscopic findings</strong> — Takayasu retinopathy or hypertensive changes.</li><li><strong>Pallor</strong> — anaemia of inflammation.</li></ul><h3 id=\"4-differential-diagnosis-two-separate-lists\">4. Differential diagnosis — two separate lists</h3><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">A. Dangerous differentials — exclude first, ranked by lethality and time-sensitivity</p><ol><li><strong>Aortic dissection</strong> — immediate mortality; can also be a complication of TAK itself.</li><li><strong>Infectious aortitis</strong> — tuberculous, syphilitic, <em>Salmonella</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, <em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>. <strong>The most important exclusion in the entire pathway</strong>, because high-dose glucocorticoids plus a DMARD or biologic in infectious aortitis can be lethal, and tuberculous aortitis is geographically co-distributed with TAK.</li><li><strong>Infective endocarditis</strong> with embolic and immune phenomena — fever, murmur, raised inflammatory markers, arterial occlusions. Overlaps closely with TAK&#39;s systemic phase.</li><li><strong>Ruptured or expanding aortic aneurysm</strong> — any cause.</li><li><strong>Cardioembolic source</strong> (myxoma, LV thrombus) producing multi-territory arterial occlusion.</li><li><strong>Antiphospholipid syndrome</strong> — arterial thrombosis in a young woman with raised inflammatory markers; management is anticoagulation, not immunosuppression alone.</li></ol></div><p><strong>B. Common differentials — ranked by prevalence</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><ol><li><strong>Premature atherosclerosis</strong> — familial hypercholesterolaemia, diabetes, CKD, post-transplant.</li><li><strong>Fibromuscular dysplasia</strong> — young women, renal and carotid arteries, hypertension. Named by EULAR as a specific differential in refractory disease <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Large-vessel giant cell arteritis</strong> — the principal mimic over 50.</li><li><strong>Behçet&#39;s disease</strong> — vascular Behçet&#39;s causes aneurysms and both arterial and venous thrombosis.</li><li><strong>IgG4-related disease</strong> — inflammatory aortitis and periaortitis.</li><li><strong>Spondyloarthritis-associated aortitis</strong> — aortic root disease and AR.</li><li><strong>Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger&#39;s)</strong> — named by EULAR as a differential in refractory disease <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Cogan syndrome</strong> — aortitis with audiovestibular and ocular inflammation.</li><li><strong>Congenital mid-aortic syndrome and coarctation.</strong></li><li><strong>Neurofibromatosis type 1 vasculopathy</strong> · <strong>radiation arteritis</strong> · <strong>relapsing polychondritis</strong> · <strong>sarcoidosis</strong> · <strong>Erdheim-Chester disease</strong> · <strong>heritable thoracic aortopathies</strong> (Marfan, Loeys-Dietz, vascular Ehlers-Danlos).</li></ol><h3 id=\"5-for-each-differential-the-discriminating-question-and-sign\">5. For each differential — the discriminating question and sign</h3><div class=\"لفافة-جدول\"><table><thead><tr><th>Differential</th><th>Ask</th><th>Sign</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aortic dissection</td><td>Was the pain maximal the instant it started, and did it move?</td><td>Inter-arm BP difference <em>with</em> a new AR murmur and abrupt-onset pulse deficit</td></tr><tr><td>Infectious aortitis</td><td>Any TB contact, prior TB treatment, cough, or high-risk sexual history?</td><td><strong>Saccular</strong> rather than concentric aortic lesion; rigor-associated fever spike</td></tr><tr><td>Infective endocarditis</td><td>Any dental or instrumentation procedure, injecting drug use, prosthetic valve?</td><td><strong>Changing</strong> murmur plus splinter haemorrhages, Osler nodes, Janeway lesions</td></tr><tr><td>Antiphospholipid syndrome</td><td>Prior venous thrombosis or recurrent pregnancy loss?</td><td>Livedo reticularis</td></tr><tr><td>Premature atherosclerosis</td><td>Smoking, family history of early coronary disease, high cholesterol?</td><td>Tendon xanthomata, xanthelasma, corneal arcus in youth</td></tr><tr><td>Fibromuscular dysplasia</td><td>Is there <strong>any</strong> systemic symptom — fever, weight loss, night sweats?</td><td>Epigastric bruit <strong>with normal ESR/CRP</strong> and no aortic wall thickening</td></tr><tr><td>Large-vessel GCA</td><td>Age at onset; new headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, visual loss?</td><td>Temporal artery thickening, tenderness, or pulselessness</td></tr><tr><td>Behçet&#39;s</td><td>Recurrent painful oral <strong>and</strong> genital ulceration?</td><td>Oral aphthae, genital scarring, pathergy</td></tr><tr><td>IgG4-related disease</td><td>Salivary or lacrimal swelling, pancreatitis, jaundice?</td><td>Bilateral submandibular gland enlargement</td></tr><tr><td>Spondyloarthritis</td><td>Inflammatory back pain, morning stiffness &gt;30 min, improves with movement?</td><td>Reduced Schober, reduced chest expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Buerger&#39;s disease</td><td>How many cigarettes, how long; fingers and toes rather than arm or calf?</td><td>Distal digital ischaemia and superficial thrombophlebitis with <strong>palpable proximal pulses</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Cogan syndrome</td><td>Hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo with red painful eyes?</td><td>Sensorineural hearing loss with interstitial keratitis</td></tr><tr><td>Heritable aortopathy</td><td>Family history of dissection, sudden death, aneurysm?</td><td>Arachnodactyly, high-arched palate, hypermobility, translucent skin</td></tr><tr><td>Neurofibromatosis 1</td><td>Family history and childhood skin lesions?</td><td>≥6 café-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, neurofibromas, Lisch nodules</td></tr><tr><td>Radiation arteritis</td><td>Prior radiotherapy, and to which field?</td><td>Stenosis confined precisely to a radiation field, with overlying telangiectasia</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"6-investigations-what-in-what-sequence-and-why\">6. Investigations — what, in what sequence, and why</h3><p><strong>Step 1 — bedside, costs nothing, do it first</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><ul><li><strong>Blood pressure in all four limbs.</strong> Identifies the inter-arm gradient (a criteria item), identifies coarctation physiology, and prevents the central-hypertension trap.</li><li><strong>Systematic pulse palpation and bruit auscultation</strong> at every site listed above.</li></ul><p><strong>Step 2 — first-line laboratory</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><ul><li><strong>ESR and CRP</strong> — elevated during active disease. <strong>Critical limitation:</strong> acute-phase reactants imperfectly correlate with disease activity on histopathology <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a>. Normal ESR and CRP do <strong>not</strong> exclude active TAK, and elevated values do not confirm it.</li><li><strong>Full blood count</strong> — normochromic normocytic anaemia and reactive thrombocytosis.</li><li><strong>Albumin</strong> — low, from the acute-phase shift.</li><li><strong>Creatinine, eGFR, urinalysis</strong> — renal artery involvement, hypertensive nephropathy, and to exclude a glomerulopathy pointing elsewhere.</li><li><strong>Liver function</strong> — baseline before methotrexate or leflunomide.</li></ul><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Step 3 — mandatory exclusion panel before any immunosuppression</p><p><strong>Blood cultures, at least three sets</strong> — endocarditis and infectious aortitis · <strong>IGRA or tuberculin skin test plus chest radiograph</strong> — required both to exclude tuberculous aortitis and as a prerequisite before TNF inhibitors or tocilizumab; in a TB-endemic setting this is not optional · <strong>syphilis serology</strong> · <strong>HIV serology</strong> · <strong>serum IgG4</strong> · <strong>ANA, ANCA, antiphospholipid antibodies</strong> — to redirect toward an alternative vasculitis or thrombophilia.</p><p><strong>There is no diagnostic autoantibody for TAK.</strong> Anti-endothelial cell antibodies are a research tool, not a clinical test. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p></div><p><strong>Step 4 — vascular imaging, the diagnostic centre of gravity</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><ul><li><strong>MRI/MRA of the aorta and its branches with contrast</strong> — shows <strong>wall thickening and mural oedema</strong> as well as the lumen, without ionising radiation; decisive in young women needing repeated imaging over decades. Expect long-segment concentric wall thickening, mural oedema and post-contrast enhancement in active disease, smooth tapering stenoses. Less good for calcification; gadolinium considerations in CKD.</li><li><strong>CT angiography</strong> — rapid, widely available, best spatial resolution; distinguishes calcified atherosclerotic plaque from inflammatory thickening; double-ring enhancement of the thickened wall. Limitation: <strong>cumulative radiation</strong> across a lifetime of surveillance in a young woman, plus iodinated contrast.</li><li><strong>Colour Doppler ultrasound</strong> of carotid, subclavian, axillary, vertebral — cheap, radiation-free, repeatable; the <strong>&quot;macaroni sign&quot;</strong> of homogeneous, circumferential, mid-echoic wall thickening. Operator-dependent; cannot assess the thoracic aorta or pulmonary arteries.</li><li><strong>¹⁸F-FDG PET/CT</strong> — assesses <strong>metabolic activity</strong> rather than anatomy; helps in the subset with normal acute-phase reactants <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a>, precisely the group in which clinical assessment fails. Cannot reliably distinguish inflammatory from atherosclerotic uptake; radiation; cost; availability.</li><li><strong>Transthoracic echocardiography</strong> — mandatory, not optional. Aortic root diameter, AR severity, LV size and function, pulmonary pressures, proximal coronary origins where visible.</li><li><strong>Catheter angiography</strong> — now largely reserved for planning or performing intervention.</li></ul><h4 id=\"diagnostic-performance-what-is-and-is-not-sourceable\">Diagnostic performance — what is and is not sourceable</h4><div class=\"كتلة جودة\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria [G]</p><p><strong>Absolute entry requirements:</strong> age ≤60 years at diagnosis <strong>and</strong> imaging evidence of large-vessel vasculitis, with classification at a cumulative score of <strong>≥5 points</strong> on a scale of 0–19 <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3,5\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3,5\">[3,5]</a></a>.</p><p><strong>Derivation and internal validation:</strong> sensitivity 89.9% and specificity 96.6% in the development data set (639 patients, 316 with TAK); sensitivity 93.8% (95% CI 88.6–97.1), specificity 99.2% (95% CI 96.7–100.0), AUC 0.97 in validation (273 patients, 146 with TAK) <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></a>.</p><p><strong>In independent external validation the specificity collapses:</strong> among 504 patients with TAK and 222 controls including 144 with extracranial GCA, the 2022 criteria had better sensitivity (95.83% vs 82.94%) and NPV than the 1990 ACR criteria, but poorer specificity (<strong>63.51% vs 90.54%</strong>), PPV, LR+, LR− and AUC <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-4\" data-مصادر=\"4\">[4]</a></a>.</p></div><div class=\"كتلة مفتاح\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">The lesson to internalise</p><p>A specificity of 99.2% in the derivation cohort and 63.51% against a real-world comparator group dominated by large-vessel GCA is the difference between a research instrument and a diagnostic test. This is exactly why EULAR states that classification criteria must not substitute for clinical diagnosis <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <strong>Do not use a score of ≥5 to diagnose a patient.</strong></p></div><p><strong>For individual imaging modalities:</strong> <strong>no reliable sensitivity, specificity, or likelihood ratio figures were located</strong> in this search <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button>. Do not quote any. If you need them, the source to interrogate is a dedicated imaging-accuracy systematic review, not a general guideline.</p><h4 id=\"assessing-disease-activity-separate-from-diagnosis\">Assessing disease activity — separate from diagnosis</h4><ul><li><strong>NIH (Kerr) criteria, 1994</strong> — active disease if at least two of four features are <strong>new or worse</strong> since the last assessment: (1) new or worsening systemic features such as fever or musculoskeletal symptoms; (2) new or worsening elevated ESR; (3) new or worsening features of vascular ischaemia or inflammation — claudication, diminished or absent pulse, bruit, carotidynia, asymmetric blood pressure in upper or lower limbs; (4) new or worsening angiographic features <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></a>. The ESR threshold used was &gt;20 mm/h <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>ITAS2010</strong> — a 44-item clinical score derived from DEI.TAK and ultimately from BVAS, scoring only abnormalities <strong>new or worsening within the past three months and attributable to active vasculitis</strong>; ITAS-A adds acute-phase reactants <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li></ul><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Their shared and disqualifying weakness</p><p>These are clinical scores that miss silent anatomical progression. During follow-up, <strong>14 of 15 patients showing vascular progression on imaging were categorised as having inactive disease according to ITAS2010</strong> <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p><p><strong>This single finding is the entire justification for routine imaging surveillance.</strong> A patient can be clinically quiescent, have a normal ESR, score zero on ITAS2010, and still be losing arterial lumen.</p></div><p>A subsequently derived tool combining high ESR (3 points), NIH criterion 1 (2 points), NIH criterion 4 (4 points), and carotidynia (3 points), with total <strong>≥5</strong> indicating active disease, achieved AUC 89.37 versus NIH criteria 77.96, ITAS2010 66.12, ITAS-ESR 75.58, and ITAS-CRP 71.34, with AUC 85.23 in validation <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button> — single-centre derivation and validation (N=100 and N=46), not in guideline use. Know it exists; do not adopt it unilaterally.</p><h3 id=\"7-gold-standard-for-definitive-diagnosis\">7. Gold standard for definitive diagnosis</h3><div class=\"كتلة دليل\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">There is no single gold standard, and this must be stated rather than finessed</p><p>Histology — granulomatous panarteritis with medial destruction and giant cells — is definitive <strong>when tissue is available</strong>, but tissue is almost never available, since biopsy of the aorta or its primary branches is only obtained incidentally at open vascular surgery.</p><p>In practice the diagnostic standard is: <strong>a compatible clinical picture, plus imaging demonstration of characteristic large-vessel wall thickening and stenosis, plus exclusion of infection and the mimics above.</strong> EULAR&#39;s overarching principle is that diagnosis rests on clinical signs and symptoms, with imaging or biopsy confirmation required for suspected GCA and TAK <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></p><p>In research and trials, physician global assessment has served as the reference standard for activity <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-12\" data-مصادر=\"12\">[12]</a></a> — that is, expert opinion. Be honest with yourself about what that means for the strength of the evidence base underneath <em>Treatment</em>.</p></div><h3 id=\"8-diagnostic-algorithm\">8. Diagnostic algorithm</h3><ol><li><strong>Trigger the thought.</strong> A patient under 40 with unexplained inflammatory illness; a young hypertensive patient; an absent or asymmetric pulse; an arterial bruit in a young person; stroke, MI, or claudication under 40; a young woman with an unexplained raised ESR.</li><li><strong>Bedside:</strong> four-limb BP; full pulse and bruit survey; cardiac examination; fundoscopy.</li><li><strong>First-line bloods:</strong> ESR, CRP, FBC, albumin, creatinine, urinalysis, LFT.</li><li><strong>Exclusion panel in parallel, not afterwards:</strong> blood cultures ×3, IGRA or TST plus chest radiograph, syphilis serology, HIV, IgG4, ANA, ANCA, antiphospholipid antibodies.</li><li><strong>Vascular imaging of the whole aorta and its primary branches</strong> — MRA preferred in the young; CTA if MRA unavailable or contraindicated. <strong>Image the whole aorta, not only the symptomatic territory</strong>, because skip lesions and silent disease are the rule.</li><li><strong>Echocardiography in every patient</strong> — root, valve, ventricle, pulmonary pressures.</li><li><strong>If imaging is equivocal or acute-phase reactants are discordant:</strong> consider FDG-PET/CT.</li><li><strong>Synthesise.</strong> Assign a Hata/Numano type, document C+ and P+ involvement, and record a baseline activity assessment (NIH criteria and ITAS2010) <em>and</em> a baseline damage map. <strong>Activity and damage are separate axes and must be tracked separately.</strong></li><li><strong>Confirm infection is excluded before the first dose of glucocorticoid.</strong> Unlike GCA, TAK is not a treat-first-confirm-later disease: in PMR or TAK, glucocorticoid initiation can await confirmed diagnosis; GCA is a medical emergency and a glucocorticoid trial is not a diagnostic test <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></li><li><strong>Refer.</strong> Suspected TAK should be referred to a specialist with appropriate expertise <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></li></ol><h3 id=\"9-if-a-skin-lesion-is-present\">9. If a skin lesion is present</h3><p>TAK is not primarily dermatological, but two cutaneous associations occur and are examinable <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button> (reported in cohort series; frequency not reliably established).</p><p><strong>Erythema nodosum</strong> — <em>Primary morphology:</em> tender, deep-seated, erythematous to violaceous <strong>nodules</strong>, 1–5 cm, without ulceration. <em>Secondary change:</em> resolves through bruise-like colour change (erythema contusiforme); no scarring. <em>Configuration:</em> discrete, scattered. <em>Distribution:</em> bilateral <strong>anterior shins</strong>, extensor surfaces. <em>Symmetry:</em> symmetrical. <em>Surface change:</em> none — smooth, intact epidermis; the pathology is septal panniculitis in the subcutis.</p><p><strong>Pyoderma gangrenosum</strong> — <em>Primary morphology:</em> begins as a tender pustule or nodule. <em>Secondary change:</em> rapidly enlarging <strong>ulcer</strong> with a violaceous, undermined, overhanging border and a purulent necrotic base; heals with cribriform scarring. <em>Configuration:</em> solitary or few; may show pathergy at sites of trauma. <em>Distribution:</em> most often lower legs. <em>Symmetry:</em> asymmetrical. <em>Surface change:</em> frank ulceration with exudate.</p><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Do not debride pyoderma gangrenosum</p><p>Pathergy worsens it. This is a common and damaging error.</p></div>"},{"معرّف":"sixth-natural-history","عنوان":"Sixth: Natural history","html":"<p><strong>Untreated trajectory</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — not a self-limiting disease. The typical course is <strong>relapsing–remitting with progressive anatomical damage</strong>, over years to decades. Sequence: inflammatory phase → progressive concentric stenosis → collateral development (which delays and masks symptoms) → target organ consequences. Because collaterals are efficient in a young patient, <strong>anatomical severity outruns symptoms</strong>, and the first clinical event may be a stroke, a myocardial infarction, or a hypertensive emergency. Death, when it occurs, is predominantly cardiovascular <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-11\" data-مصادر=\"11\">[11]</a></a>. Spontaneous permanent remission of inflammation (&quot;burnt-out&quot; disease) does occur, but the accumulated stenosis and aneurysm do not reverse. <strong>Remission is not recovery.</strong></p><p><strong>Treated trajectory</strong> — most patients achieve clinical and biochemical remission on glucocorticoids. <strong>Relapse on tapering is common, and glucocorticoid dependence is a defining problem of the disease</strong> — why EULAR recommends a non-biologic DMARD for <strong>all</strong> TAK patients rather than for selected ones <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><div class=\"كتلة مفتاح\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">The central and most clinically important fact</p><p>Anatomical progression continues in a substantial minority of patients who appear to be in clinical and biochemical remission <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button> <strong>Clinical quiescence is not evidence of arrested disease.</strong> Long-term outcome is determined less by control of inflammation than by the damage already sustained at diagnosis — hence the value of early recognition and the cost of diagnostic delay.</p></div><p><strong>No reliable pooled figure for remission rate, relapse rate, or survival was located</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button>. Cohort estimates vary widely by era, region, and treatment. Do not quote one.</p>"},{"معرّف":"seventh-complications-and-sequelae","عنوان":"Seventh: Complications and sequelae","html":"<h3 id=\"complications-of-the-disease\">Complications of the disease</h3><div class=\"لفافة-جدول\"><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Complication</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Approximate timing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vascular</td><td>Critical stenosis or occlusion</td><td>Fibrostenotic remodelling</td><td>Months to years</td></tr><tr><td>Vascular</td><td>Aneurysm, dissection, rupture</td><td>Medial elastic destruction</td><td>Years; can be early in aneurysmal phenotype</td></tr><tr><td>Cardiac</td><td>Aortic regurgitation, LV volume overload, heart failure</td><td>Aortic root dilatation</td><td>Years</td></tr><tr><td>Cardiac</td><td>Angina, myocardial infarction</td><td>Coronary ostial stenosis</td><td>Any time; may be the presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Cardiac</td><td>Pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure</td><td>Pulmonary artery stenosis</td><td>Years; frequently missed</td></tr><tr><td>Renal</td><td>Renovascular and malignant hypertension</td><td>Renal artery stenosis, RAAS activation</td><td>Early, often at presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Renal</td><td>Chronic kidney disease</td><td>Renal artery stenosis plus hypertensive nephrosclerosis</td><td>Years</td></tr><tr><td>Neurological</td><td>TIA, ischaemic stroke</td><td>Carotid/vertebral stenosis, thromboembolism, haemodynamic failure</td><td>Any time; may be the presentation</td></tr><tr><td>Neurological</td><td>Subclavian steal symptoms</td><td>Flow reversal in vertebral artery</td><td>With established proximal subclavian disease</td></tr><tr><td>Ocular</td><td>Hypoperfusive retinopathy, visual loss</td><td>Chronic ocular hypoperfusion; hypertensive retinopathy</td><td>Years</td></tr><tr><td>GI</td><td>Chronic mesenteric ischaemia</td><td>Coeliac/mesenteric stenosis</td><td>Years; collaterals often protective</td></tr><tr><td>Limb</td><td>Rest pain, ulceration, rarely gangrene</td><td>Severe distal ischaemia</td><td>Late; collaterals usually protective</td></tr><tr><td>Obstetric</td><td>Pre-eclampsia, fetal growth restriction, preterm birth</td><td>Placental hypoperfusion; maternal hypertension</td><td>Pregnancy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"complications-of-treatment-e\">Complications of treatment <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></h3><p><strong>Glucocorticoids</strong> — the dominant iatrogenic burden, precisely because these are young patients facing years of exposure.</p><ul><li><em>Early (weeks to months):</em> hyperglycaemia and steroid-induced diabetes, hypertension, weight gain and Cushingoid change, mood disturbance and insomnia, infection.</li><li><em>Intermediate (months):</em> osteoporosis, myopathy, cataract, glaucoma, HPA suppression, poor wound healing.</li><li><em>Late (months to years):</em> avascular necrosis of the femoral head, permanent adrenal insufficiency risk on abrupt withdrawal, growth suppression in children.</li><li>Limiting this exposure is an explicit overarching aim of the ACR/VF guideline <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\">[15]</a></a>.</li></ul><p><strong>Methotrexate</strong> — hepatotoxicity, myelosuppression, mucositis, rarely pneumonitis; <strong>teratogenic</strong>, requires effective contraception and cessation before conception in both sexes.</p><p><strong>Azathioprine</strong> — myelosuppression (test <strong>TPMT</strong> and, in East and South Asian populations, <strong>NUDT15</strong> before starting), hepatotoxicity, hypersensitivity, long-term malignancy risk; interacts catastrophically with allopurinol.</p><p><strong>Leflunomide</strong> — hepatotoxicity, hypertension, diarrhoea, peripheral neuropathy; <strong>teratogenic with a very long half-life</strong> requiring cholestyramine washout.</p><p><strong>Mycophenolate mofetil</strong> — cytopenias, diarrhoea, infection; <strong>teratogenic</strong>.</p><p><strong>Cyclophosphamide</strong> — infertility, haemorrhagic cystitis, bladder malignancy, myelosuppression, infection. Now rarely used in TAK; reserve for organ-threatening refractory disease under specialist direction.</p><p><strong>TNF inhibitors</strong> — <strong>tuberculosis reactivation</strong> (screen before starting, without exception), hepatitis B reactivation, serious bacterial infection, demyelination, drug-induced lupus.</p><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Tocilizumab — a monitoring hazard, not merely a toxicity</p><p>IL-6 receptor blockade <strong>abolishes the CRP response</strong>, so CRP becomes uninformative for both disease activity <strong>and</strong> intercurrent infection. <strong>A patient on tocilizumab can have a normal CRP with a perforated colon.</strong></p><p>Other effects: neutropenia, transaminitis, dyslipidaemia, gastrointestinal perforation (higher risk with diverticular disease), injection-site or infusion reactions.</p></div><p><strong>Vascular intervention</strong> — restenosis, anastomotic pseudoaneurysm, graft occlusion, periprocedural stroke. Open interventions carry higher primary patency rates; endovascular approaches carry lower stroke risk <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button> Restenosis is markedly more likely if intervention is performed during active disease — the entire reason for the timing recommendation below.</p>"},{"معرّف":"eighth-treatment","عنوان":"Eighth: Treatment","html":"<div class=\"كتلة دليل\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Rule B6 declaration — two bodies, and they are not identical</p><p><strong>According to the 2025 EULAR recommendations for the management of polymyalgia rheumatica and primary large vessel vasculitis</strong> (presented at the EULAR 2026 congress, published in <em>Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases</em>, superseding the 2018 EULAR LVV recommendations) <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,2\">[1,2]</a></a>…</p><p><strong>and according to the 2021 ACR/Vasculitis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Giant Cell Arteritis and Takayasu Arteritis</strong> <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6,7,15\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6,7,15\">[6,7,15]</a></a>…</p></div><div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Rule B7 — guideline divergence, named explicitly [G]</p><p><strong>1. DMARD for all versus DMARD for some.</strong> EULAR 2025 states that non-biologic DMARDs should be given to <strong>all</strong> patients with TAK <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. The ACR/VF 2021 framing is initial treatment with high-dose oral glucocorticoids for active severe TAK, with IV pulse glucocorticoids followed by high-dose oral glucocorticoids for life- or organ-threatening disease <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></a>, with non-glucocorticoid immunosuppression added. <strong>I could not retrieve the exact ACR/VF wording on universal DMARD use.</strong> Treat the EULAR position as the more recent and more universal; verify the ACR/VF text before relying on the contrast.</p><p><strong>2. Imaging in monitoring.</strong> EULAR 2025 makes routine imaging for <strong>both activity and damage</strong> mandatory in all TAK patients, and states that relapse diagnosis in TAK is substantially based on imaging in conjunction with clinical and laboratory assessment — distinguishing it from PMR and GCA where imaging plays a more supportive role <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</p><p><strong>3. Imaging in diagnosis more broadly.</strong> The ACR/VF guideline explicitly notes that its recommendations on temporal artery imaging differ from EULAR&#39;s <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-15\" data-مصادر=\"15\">[15]</a></a>. The underlying difference — US preference for histology, European preference for imaging as sufficient — colours both documents.</p><p><strong>4. Upadacitinib.</strong> EULAR 2025 adds upadacitinib as an option <strong>in GCA only</strong>; for TAK the recommendation is tocilizumab or TNF inhibitors for relapsing or refractory disease despite conventional DMARD therapy <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <strong>Do not extrapolate upadacitinib from GCA to TAK.</strong> The evidence base does not support it and the guideline deliberately did not.</p></div><div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Which position will your local practice follow? (Rules B7, B8)</p><p>If you are training in South or East Asia, the Middle East, or Africa, local practice is more likely to align with the <strong>EULAR structure</strong> (glucocorticoid plus a conventional DMARD in everyone, escalating to a biologic only if available), because the ACR/VF pathway&#39;s conditional recommendations assume ready access to tocilizumab and TNF inhibitors. The Chinese guideline <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\">[9]</a></a> is a further regionally adapted option. <strong>Confirm with your senior registrar which document your unit works from before you write anything on a drug chart.</strong></p></div><h3 id=\"pharmacological-therapy\">Pharmacological therapy</h3><h4 id=\"glucocorticoid-the-backbone-g\">Glucocorticoid — the backbone <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></h4><p><strong>Prednisolone</strong> — systemic glucocorticoid; binds the cytosolic glucocorticoid receptor, which translocates to the nucleus and transrepresses NF-κB and AP-1, suppressing transcription of IL-1, IL-6, TNF-α, and adhesion molecules.</p><ul><li><strong>Dosage:</strong> in newly diagnosed active TAK, <strong>40–60 mg/day</strong>, tapered to <strong>15–20 mg/day within 2–3 months</strong>, with the goal of stopping <strong>within 12–18 months</strong> <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></li><li><strong>Relapse:</strong> major relapses are managed as new-onset disease; minor relapses require escalation to at least the last effective dose with individualised tapering <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Organ- or life-threatening disease:</strong> ACR/VF conditionally supports IV pulse glucocorticoids followed by high-dose oral glucocorticoids <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Children:</strong> alternative steroid dosing regimens, for example IV pulse glucocorticoids with low daily oral dosing, may be preferred <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></a> — the rationale being growth suppression.</li><li><strong>Contraindications:</strong> no absolute contraindication in organ-threatening vasculitis, but <strong>untreated systemic infection, active tuberculosis, and untreated infectious aortitis are effectively absolute</strong> until treated. Relative: poorly controlled diabetes, severe osteoporosis, active peptic ulceration, psychosis.</li><li><strong>Duration:</strong> aiming for 12–18 months; in practice a substantial proportion remain glucocorticoid-dependent.</li><li><strong>Co-prescribe from day one:</strong> calcium and vitamin D, bone protection assessment, gastroprotection where indicated, and <em>Pneumocystis</em> prophylaxis consideration if on high-dose glucocorticoid plus another immunosuppressant.</li><li><strong>Never stop abruptly</strong> after more than roughly three weeks of suppressive dosing — HPA axis suppression risks adrenal crisis.</li></ul><h4 id=\"conventional-synthetic-dmards-in-all-patients-per-eular-2025-g\">Conventional synthetic DMARDs — in all patients per EULAR 2025 <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></h4><p>Comparative efficacy between agents has <strong>not</strong> been established by adequate trials <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a></a>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></p><p><strong>Methotrexate</strong> — folate antagonist; inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and, more relevantly at low dose, AICAR transformylase, raising extracellular adenosine with anti-inflammatory effect. <em>Contraindications:</em> pregnancy and conception planning, significant liver disease, alcohol excess, severe renal impairment, cytopenias. <em>Dosage:</em> once-weekly oral or subcutaneous, titrated upward, <strong>with mandatory folic acid on a different day</strong>. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> <em>Adverse effects:</em> nausea, mucositis, transaminitis, cytopenias, rarely pneumonitis. <em>Monitoring:</em> FBC, LFT, creatinine at baseline then per local protocol. <em>Duration:</em> continued through remission; withdrawal usually precipitates relapse.</p><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Fatal error to avoid</p><p><strong>Never prescribe methotrexate daily. Weekly dosing only.</strong></p></div><p><strong>Azathioprine</strong> — purine analogue; 6-thioguanine nucleotides incorporate into DNA and inhibit lymphocyte proliferation. <em>Contraindications:</em> TPMT deficiency, concurrent allopurinol or febuxostat without dose reduction, prior hypersensitivity. <em>Dosage:</em> weight-based daily oral. <strong>Check TPMT, and NUDT15 in East and South Asian patients, before the first dose.</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> <em>Adverse effects:</em> myelosuppression, hepatotoxicity, hypersensitivity syndrome, long-term lymphoma and non-melanoma skin cancer risk. <strong>Preferred conventional agent in pregnancy planning</strong> among the cytotoxics, being the least teratogenic of this group. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></p><p><strong>Mycophenolate mofetil</strong> — inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, selectively depleting guanosine nucleotides in lymphocytes. <em>Contraindications:</em> pregnancy (<strong>teratogenic — high-grade risk</strong>), prior intolerance. <em>Adverse effects:</em> diarrhoea, cytopenias, infection.</p><p><strong>Leflunomide</strong> — inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, blocking pyrimidine synthesis in activated lymphocytes. <em>Contraindications:</em> pregnancy (teratogenic, long half-life requiring cholestyramine washout), significant liver disease. <em>Adverse effects:</em> diarrhoea, hypertension, hepatotoxicity, peripheral neuropathy, alopecia.</p><p><strong>Cyclophosphamide</strong> — reserved for organ-threatening refractory disease; specialist use only. Fertility preservation must be discussed before the first dose in a young patient.</p><h4 id=\"biologics-for-relapsing-or-refractory-disease-g\">Biologics — for relapsing or refractory disease <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></h4><p>For relapsing or refractory disease despite conventional DMARD therapy, <strong>tocilizumab or TNF inhibitors</strong> should be considered <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</p><p><strong>Tocilizumab</strong> — humanised monoclonal antibody against the IL-6 receptor; blocks both membrane-bound and soluble IL-6R signalling, interrupting the IL-6/JAK/STAT3 axis that drives the acute-phase response and Th17 differentiation. <em>Contraindications:</em> active serious infection, active tuberculosis, neutropenia below local threshold, significant transaminitis. Relative: diverticular disease. <em>Route:</em> intravenous or subcutaneous. <em>Monitoring hazard:</em> see red box above — <strong>CRP cannot be used to detect either flare or infection on this drug.</strong> Monitor with symptoms, examination, and imaging. <em>Duration:</em> while effective; optimal duration unestablished <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button>.</p><p><strong>TNF inhibitors — infliximab or adalimumab</strong> — bind soluble and membrane TNF-α, preventing engagement of TNFR1/TNFR2 and interrupting macrophage activation and granuloma maintenance. <em>Contraindications:</em> <strong>active or latent untreated tuberculosis</strong> (screen before every course, without exception), active serious infection, untreated hepatitis B, moderate-to-severe heart failure (NYHA III–IV), demyelinating disease. <em>Monitoring:</em> TB and hepatitis screening at baseline; FBC, LFT; clinical vigilance for infection.</p><p><strong>Upadacitinib</strong> — <strong>not recommended for TAK.</strong> EULAR 2025 introduced it for GCA only <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. Do not extrapolate.</p><h4 id=\"adjunctive-cardiovascular-therapy-g-for-the-principle-1-e-for-the-pharmacology\">Adjunctive cardiovascular therapy <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button> for the principle <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>; <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> for the pharmacology</h4><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">The two antihypertensive errors that cause harm</p><p><strong>1. Treating to the reading from an arm supplied by a stenosed subclavian artery</strong> — this systematically underestimates central pressure and leaves severe hypertension untreated. Always use the highest of the four limb pressures, and where all four are compromised, obtain central pressure.</p><p><strong>2. ACE inhibitors or ARBs in bilateral renal artery stenosis or stenosis to a single functioning kidney</strong> — angiotensin II is maintaining the glomerular filtration gradient through efferent arteriolar constriction; blocking it precipitates acute kidney injury. Check renal artery anatomy before prescribing, and check creatinine within days of starting.</p><p><strong>Calcium channel blockers</strong> (e.g. amlodipine) are usually a safer first choice where renal artery anatomy is uncertain or bilateral disease is present.</p></div><ul><li><strong>Statin</strong> — for cardiovascular risk reduction in a population with accelerated arterial disease and long glucocorticoid exposure. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button> for the principle of CV risk mitigation <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Aspirin</strong> — often used, particularly with cerebrovascular or coronary involvement. <strong>The evidence base in TAK specifically is weak; I could not retrieve a guideline recommendation on antiplatelet therapy in TAK.</strong> Do not present it as guideline-mandated. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></li></ul><h3 id=\"surgical-and-procedural-therapy-g\">Surgical and procedural therapy <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></h3><div class=\"كتلة دليل\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Timing is the single most important principle</p><p>Elective vascular interventions should be performed <strong>only during stable remission</strong>, with urgent vascular referral for dissection or critical ischaemia <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</p><p>ACR/VF concurs: for worsening signs of limb or organ ischaemia, delay surgical intervention until the disease is quiescent rather than operating during active disease; and for patients with active TAK undergoing vascular surgical intervention for a disease complication, periprocedural high-dose glucocorticoids are conditionally recommended <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></a>.</p><p><strong>Modality trade-off to state to the patient:</strong> open interventions carry higher primary patency rates; endovascular approaches carry lower stroke risk <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</p></div><div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Where ACR/VF prefers medical management over intervention [G]</p><p><strong>Renovascular hypertension with renal artery stenosis</strong> — conditionally recommend medical management over surgical intervention. <strong>Stenosis of a cranial or cervical vessel without clinical symptoms</strong> — conditionally recommend medical management over surgical intervention <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></a>.</p><p><strong>An asymptomatic carotid stenosis in TAK is not automatically an indication to intervene.</strong> This runs against instinct and is worth remembering.</p></div><p><strong>Genuine intervention indications</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button> — critical or threatened limb ischaemia; symptomatic cerebrovascular stenosis with recurrent events despite medical therapy; severe coarctation-like aortic narrowing with uncontrollable hypertension; renal artery stenosis with refractory hypertension or deteriorating renal function after medical therapy has failed; aneurysm meeting size or growth thresholds; severe aortic regurgitation; symptomatic coronary ostial stenosis.</p><p><strong>Emergency indications requiring immediate referral</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button> — arterial vessel dissection or critical vascular ischaemia requires urgent vascular team referral <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</p><h3 id=\"physiotherapy-and-rehabilitation-l\">Physiotherapy and rehabilitation <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button></h3><ul><li>Structured aerobic exercise and cardiac rehabilitation where cardiac or cerebrovascular involvement exists. <strong>No TAK-specific rehabilitation trial evidence was located</strong> <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button> — extrapolated from general cardiovascular rehabilitation.</li><li>Avoid heavy isometric and Valsalva-generating exertion where there is significant aortic dilatation, aneurysm, or severe uncontrolled hypertension, since these produce abrupt rises in aortic wall stress. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li>Resistance and weight-bearing exercise for glucocorticoid-induced bone loss and myopathy. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li>Supervised exercise therapy for claudication where the limiting lesion is not critical. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li></ul><h3 id=\"lifestyle-modification-and-patient-counselling-g\">Lifestyle modification and patient counselling <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></h3><ul><li>Screen for cardiovascular comorbidities, with prophylactic and lifestyle interventions to mitigate risk <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a> — blood pressure, lipids, glucose, weight, smoking.</li><li><strong>Absolute smoking cessation.</strong> Smoking compounds arterial damage and is the discriminating feature of the main mimic (Buerger&#39;s).</li><li>Patients require access to disease education including warning symptoms and treatment complications <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. Teach explicitly: symptoms of stroke and TIA; new or changed chest pain; sudden severe tearing chest or back pain; new limb pain, pallor, or coldness; fever while immunosuppressed.</li><li><strong>Steroid card and adrenal crisis education.</strong> Never stop glucocorticoid abruptly; increase during intercurrent illness per local sick-day rules.</li><li><strong>Contraception and pregnancy planning.</strong> Methotrexate, mycophenolate, and leflunomide are teratogenic. Pregnancy must be planned during remission, on a compatible drug, with multidisciplinary obstetric and rheumatology input.</li><li><strong>Vaccination.</strong> Complete inactivated vaccinations before immunosuppression where possible; <strong>live vaccines are contraindicated</strong> on significant immunosuppression.</li><li><strong>Tuberculosis screening before any biologic</strong>, repeated per local policy.</li></ul><h3 id=\"monitoring-g\">Monitoring <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></h3><p>Regular monitoring should incorporate symptoms, clinical findings, acute phase markers, and imaging throughout the disease course, with imaging used to assess <strong>both activity and damage</strong> in all TAK patients <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</p><ul><li><strong>Every visit:</strong> symptom review against the NIH criterion domains; <strong>four-limb blood pressure</strong>; pulse and bruit survey; cardiac examination; weight; glucocorticoid toxicity review.</li><li><strong>Laboratory:</strong> ESR and CRP — <strong>imperfectly correlated with histological activity</strong> <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a> and <strong>uninterpretable on tocilizumab</strong>. Plus drug-specific monitoring (FBC, LFT, creatinine).</li><li><strong>Imaging:</strong> routinely, per EULAR, with modality chosen for the accessible territory and cumulative radiation minimised in young patients — Doppler ultrasound for accessible neck and arm vessels, MRA for the aorta, reserving CTA and PET/CT for specific questions.</li><li><strong>Echocardiography:</strong> serially, tracking aortic root diameter, AR severity, LV function, pulmonary pressures.</li><li><strong>Targets:</strong> absence of new or worsening symptoms; normalised acute-phase reactants where interpretable; <strong>no anatomical progression on imaging</strong>; blood pressure to guideline target measured from a valid limb; glucocorticoid at or below the tapering trajectory.</li></ul><h3 id=\"expected-response-and-the-meaning-of-non-response-g\">Expected response and the meaning of non-response <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button></h3><p><strong>Expected:</strong> systemic symptoms and acute-phase reactants improve within days to weeks of adequate glucocorticoid. Vascular wall oedema and enhancement regress over weeks to months. <strong>Established stenosis does not reverse</strong> — do not expect it to, and do not interpret persistent stenosis as treatment failure.</p><div class=\"كتلة مفتاح\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Non-response means the diagnosis must be revisited, not that the dose should be escalated</p><p>Refractory TAK requires diagnostic re-evaluation, with differentials including <strong>Buerger&#39;s disease and fibromuscular dysplasia</strong>; refractory PMR, GCA or TAK should have the diagnosis re-evaluated and be considered for referral to a specialist centre <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</p><p>Also reconsider: undiagnosed <strong>infectious aortitis</strong> (the treatment is antimicrobial, and immunosuppression is making it worse), non-adherence, and progression of fixed damage being misread as active inflammation.</p></div><h3 id=\"rule-b8-local-applicability-and-practical-substitutions\">Rule B8 — local applicability and practical substitutions</h3><div class=\"لفافة-جدول\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unavailable</th><th>Practical alternative that is actually available</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tocilizumab</td><td>Prednisolone plus methotrexate or azathioprine, optimised to maximum tolerated dose; consider leflunomide or mycophenolate before conceding refractoriness</td></tr><tr><td>TNF inhibitors</td><td>As above. If a biologic can be obtained at all, biosimilar infliximab is usually the cheapest route</td></tr><tr><td>¹⁸F-FDG PET/CT</td><td>Serial colour Doppler ultrasound of carotid, subclavian, and axillary arteries plus ESR/CRP; CTA at longer intervals for the aorta</td></tr><tr><td>MRA</td><td>CTA — accepting the radiation trade-off and explicitly documenting cumulative dose in a young woman</td></tr><tr><td>High-sensitivity CRP</td><td>ESR alone is acceptable for trend monitoring; the NIH criteria were built around ESR &gt;20 mm/h <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-13\" data-مصادر=\"13\">[13]</a></a></td></tr><tr><td>TPMT/NUDT15 genotyping</td><td>Start azathioprine at a low dose with intensive early FBC monitoring; the NUDT15 variant is common in East and South Asian populations, so this substitution carries real risk and should be a senior decision</td></tr><tr><td>Central aortic pressure measurement</td><td>Four-limb cuff pressure, using the highest value; document the limitation</td></tr><tr><td>Specialist vascular surgery and hybrid theatre</td><td>Early referral and transfer planning; <strong>do not attempt elective intervention locally during active disease</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"كتلة تحذير\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Additionally, in a tuberculosis-endemic setting</p><p>The threshold for excluding tuberculous aortitis before immunosuppression must be <strong>higher, not lower</strong>, than in the guideline text — and IGRA availability is often the limiting factor. Where IGRA is unavailable, tuberculin skin testing plus chest radiography plus a deliberate clinical assessment is the fallback, and this specific decision should involve a senior.</p></div>"},{"معرّف":"ninth-red-flags","عنوان":"Ninth: Red flags","html":"<h3 id=\"symptoms-and-signs-that-must-never-be-dismissed-e\">Symptoms and signs that must never be dismissed <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></h3><div class=\"لفافة-جدول\"><table><thead><tr><th>Finding</th><th>What it means</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sudden severe tearing chest or interscapular pain, maximal at onset</td><td><strong>Aortic dissection</strong></td><td>Immediate imaging and vascular/cardiothoracic referral</td></tr><tr><td>New focal neurological deficit or amaurosis</td><td>Stroke or TIA from carotid/vertebral disease or embolism</td><td>Stroke pathway activation</td></tr><tr><td>Dizziness or drop attacks on arm exertion</td><td>Subclavian steal with posterior circulation compromise</td><td>Urgent vascular imaging</td></tr><tr><td>New or crescendo chest pain in a patient under 40 with TAK</td><td><strong>Coronary ostial stenosis; myocardial infarction</strong></td><td>ECG, troponin, ACS pathway</td></tr><tr><td>Rest pain, pallor, pulselessness, paraesthesia, paralysis, cold limb</td><td><strong>Acute or critical limb ischaemia</strong></td><td>Urgent vascular referral</td></tr><tr><td>BP ≥180/120 with headache, visual change, chest pain, breathlessness, or confusion</td><td>Hypertensive emergency, usually renovascular</td><td>Immediate assessment for target organ damage</td></tr><tr><td>Rapidly enlarging pulsatile abdominal mass with pain</td><td><strong>Aneurysm expansion or rupture</strong></td><td>Immediate imaging and surgical referral</td></tr><tr><td>New early diastolic murmur, widening pulse pressure, breathlessness</td><td>Progressive aortic regurgitation from root dilatation</td><td>Urgent echocardiography</td></tr><tr><td>Fever on glucocorticoid plus a DMARD or biologic</td><td><strong>Serious infection, and the inflammatory response may be blunted</strong></td><td>Treat as neutropenic-sepsis-equivalent until proven otherwise</td></tr><tr><td>Fever plus new murmur</td><td>Infective endocarditis, or infectious aortitis</td><td>Blood cultures before antibiotics; <strong>do not escalate immunosuppression</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Breathlessness with raised JVP and loud P2</td><td>Pulmonary artery involvement with pulmonary hypertension</td><td>Echocardiography; specialist referral</td></tr><tr><td>Progressive visual loss</td><td>Hypoperfusive or hypertensive retinopathy</td><td>Urgent ophthalmology</td></tr><tr><td>Worsening hypertension or proteinuria in pregnancy</td><td>Pre-eclampsia superimposed on TAK</td><td>Urgent obstetric assessment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"drug-contraindications-capable-of-killing-the-patient\">Drug contraindications capable of killing the patient</h3><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">The twelve [E]</p><ol><li><strong>High-dose glucocorticoids or a biologic in undiagnosed infectious aortitis, active tuberculosis, or infective endocarditis.</strong> Disseminated infection under immunosuppression. <strong>Exclude infection before the first dose.</strong> This is why TAK, unlike GCA, is not treated before the diagnosis is confirmed <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>TNF inhibitor without prior tuberculosis screening.</strong> Reactivation with miliary or disseminated TB.</li><li><strong>ACE inhibitor or ARB in bilateral renal artery stenosis, or stenosis to a single functioning kidney.</strong> AKI from loss of the angiotensin II-dependent filtration gradient.</li><li><strong>Abrupt cessation of glucocorticoid after prolonged therapy.</strong> Adrenal crisis.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate prescribed daily instead of weekly.</strong> Fatal pancytopenia and mucositis.</li><li><strong>Azathioprine with allopurinol or febuxostat without dose reduction.</strong> Xanthine oxidase inhibition blocks 6-mercaptopurine catabolism → profound myelosuppression.</li><li><strong>Azathioprine at full dose in undetected TPMT or NUDT15 deficiency.</strong> Profound myelosuppression.</li><li><strong>Live vaccine during significant immunosuppression.</strong> Disseminated vaccine-strain infection.</li><li><strong>Methotrexate, mycophenolate, or leflunomide in pregnancy or without effective contraception.</strong> Teratogenicity and fetal loss.</li><li><strong>Treating blood pressure to the reading from a stenosed limb.</strong> Either under-treatment of severe central hypertension, or iatrogenic central hypotension with cerebral and renal hypoperfusion.</li><li><strong>Elective vascular intervention during active disease.</strong> Restenosis, anastomotic pseudoaneurysm, graft failure. Contraindicated by both bodies <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1,6\">[1,6]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Debriding pyoderma gangrenosum.</strong> Pathergy with catastrophic wound extension.</li></ol></div><h3 id=\"time-critical-windows\">Time-critical windows</h3><ul><li><strong>Aortic dissection:</strong> immediate. Mortality rises with every hour untreated. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>Acute ischaemic stroke:</strong> thrombolysis and thrombectomy windows are measured in hours and are defined by the current AHA/ASA or ESO stroke guideline, <strong>not by any vasculitis guideline</strong> — check the stroke protocol, not this document. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button> by reference.</li><li><strong>Acute limb ischaemia:</strong> revascularisation within hours; the appearance of paralysis marks probable non-salvageability. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"E\" title=\"إجماع راسخ — established consensus\">E</button></li><li><strong>STEMI:</strong> primary PCI within the interval specified by the current ACS guideline. <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"G\" title=\"مشتقّ من دليل — guideline-derived\">G</button> by reference.</li><li><strong>Arterial dissection or critical vascular ischaemia in known TAK:</strong> urgent vascular team referral — <strong>no defined numeric interval given</strong> in the recommendation <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>.</li><li><strong>Suspected cranial GCA (for contrast, because you will be asked):</strong> urgent specialist referral within <strong>24 hours</strong>, with glucocorticoid commenced immediately without waiting for confirmatory investigations <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>. <strong>TAK does not carry this 24-hour rule</strong> — the distinction is deliberate and examinable.</li></ul><div class=\"كتلة إنذار\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Rule B11 — escalation boundary</p><p>Three decisions in this disease should not be made from an AI answer, a textbook, or a guideline flowchart alone, because the evidence is <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button> and being wrong causes irreversible harm:</p><ol><li><strong>Whether to start immunosuppression before infection is definitively excluded</strong> in a TB-endemic setting with limited diagnostics.</li><li><strong>Whether and when to intervene on a stenosis</strong>, and by which modality — the timing rule is clear, the individual patient&#39;s balance of stroke risk against patency is not.</li><li><strong>Whether apparent refractoriness is active disease, fixed damage, an alternative diagnosis, or occult infection</strong> — this determines whether the next step is escalation, de-escalation, or antimicrobials, and getting it backwards is dangerous.</li></ol><p><strong>These require a senior decision, not an AI answer.</strong></p></div><div class=\"كتلة اختبار\" data-اختبار><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Self-test — 10 questions<span class=\"اختبار-عدّاد\" data-عدّاد></span></p><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"0\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">1</span><span>Explain, from the anatomy of the arterial wall&#39;s blood supply, why Takayasu arteritis targets the aorta and its primary branches, spares small arteries, and produces wall thickening before luminal narrowing — and state the practical imaging consequence of that mechanism.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"1\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">2</span><span>A 26-year-old woman has TAK with bilateral subclavian stenosis. Both arm pressures read 108/68 mmHg. She has LV hypertrophy on echocardiography and proteinuria. Explain the discrepancy mechanistically, and state what you must do before prescribing or withholding antihypertensive therapy.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"2\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">3</span><span>State the two absolute entry requirements of the 2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria for TAK and the score threshold. Then explain why a specificity of 99.2% in the validation dataset and 63.51% against an extracranial-GCA comparator group means these criteria must not be used to diagnose a patient.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"3\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">4</span><span>Give the four NIH (Kerr) criteria for active TAK, state how many must be new or worsening, and then explain — citing the ITAS2010 follow-up finding — why satisfying none of them does not permit you to reduce immunosuppression without imaging.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"4\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">5</span><span>Name the six Hata/Numano angiographic types by the segments each involves, and state which is most common. Then explain why the pooled prevalence figures for these types should not be applied to your own patient population.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"5\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">6</span><span>Compare the EULAR position on when to start glucocorticoids in suspected TAK with the position in suspected cranial GCA. Explain the pathophysiological reason the two diseases are treated differently in this respect.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"6\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">7</span><span>A patient with TAK on tocilizumab presents with abdominal pain and a CRP of 3 mg/L. Explain in mechanistic terms why the CRP is worthless here, and name the specific complication of tocilizumab you must actively exclude.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"7\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">8</span><span>List four drug-related errors in TAK management that can kill the patient, giving the mechanism of harm for each.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"8\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">9</span><span>A 30-year-old man with known TAK, in clinical remission on prednisolone 7.5 mg and methotrexate, has an asymptomatic 70% left internal carotid stenosis found on surveillance ultrasound. State what both the ACR/VF 2021 and EULAR 2025 documents would support doing, and give the reasoning behind the timing principle.</span></label><label class=\"سؤال\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-س=\"9\"><span class=\"سؤال-رقم\">10</span><span>Distinguish Takayasu arteritis from fibromuscular dysplasia and from thromboangiitis obliterans, giving for each mimic one discriminating history question, one discriminating physical sign, and one discriminating imaging feature.</span></label><p class=\"اختبار-ملحوظة\">الأجوبة محجوبة عمدًا — استرجاعُها من ذاكرتك هو التمرين.</p></div><div class=\"كتلة تحقّق\"><p class=\"كتلة-عنوان\">Verification footer</p><p><strong>Guidelines used</strong></p><ul><li>2025 EULAR recommendations for the management of polymyalgia rheumatica and primary large vessel vasculitis (presented EULAR 2026, published <em>Ann Rheum Dis</em> 2026; supersedes the 2018 EULAR LVV recommendations) — accessed via secondary summary <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a> and published abstract <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a></a>.</li><li>2021 ACR/Vasculitis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Giant Cell Arteritis and Takayasu Arteritis — accessed via secondary summary <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6\">[6]</a></a> and guideline landing pages <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7,15\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-7\" data-مصادر=\"7,15\">[7,15]</a></a>.</li><li>2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria for Takayasu arteritis (<em>Ann Rheum Dis</em> 2022;81:1654-1660) <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-3\" data-مصادر=\"3\">[3]</a></a>.</li><li>Chinese guideline for the diagnosis and treatment of Takayasu&#39;s arteritis (2023) <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-9\" data-مصادر=\"9\">[9]</a></a> — noted as existing; content not extracted beyond epidemiology.</li><li>Stroke and ACS time windows: <strong>deferred to the current AHA/ASA stroke and 2025 ACC/AHA ACS guidelines</strong>, not specified here.</li></ul><p><strong>Generated:</strong> 04 August 2026.</p><p><strong>Could not verify</strong></p><ul><li><strong>No project knowledge files are present.</strong> Every claim above is <strong>[outside project knowledge]</strong> and none is sourced to rotation material.</li><li><strong>The primary text of the 2025 EULAR recommendations.</strong> All specific glucocorticoid doses, tapering targets, and TAK recommendation wording derive from a secondary conference summary <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a> whose author discloses AI assistance, corroborated only by the published abstract <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a></a>. <strong>Verify every dose and interval against the primary article before prescribing.</strong></li><li><strong>Document naming.</strong> The recommendations are titled &quot;2025 EULAR recommendations&quot; but were presented in June 2026 and published in 2026. One source describes 5 overarching principles and 12 recommendations <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-1\" data-مصادر=\"1\">[1]</a></a>; the published abstract states 4 overarching principles, 12 recommendations, and 2 quality indicators <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-2\" data-مصادر=\"2\">[2]</a></a>. <strong>These two accounts conflict on the number of overarching principles.</strong> Unresolved; the primary text governs.</li><li><strong>The exact ACR/VF 2021 wording on universal DMARD use in TAK.</strong> The divergence claim rests on secondary summaries <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6,15\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-6\" data-مصادر=\"6,15\">[6,15]</a></a>, not guideline text. Confirm before asserting the contrast.</li><li><strong>Symptom and sign frequencies.</strong> No reliable pooled frequency data located. The only retrieved dataset was a review of 43 published case reports <a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\"><a class=\"استشهاد\" href=\"#مصدر-14\" data-مصادر=\"14\">[14]</a></a>, deliberately not presented as frequencies. The template&#39;s &quot;percentage of each symptom, ranked most to least common&quot; <strong>cannot be completed honestly for this disease.</strong></li><li><strong>Type IV pooled angiographic prevalence</strong> — not retrieved; not interpolated <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button>.</li><li><strong>Mortality rate, survival, remission rate, relapse rate</strong> — no reliable pooled figures located <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button>. Do not quote any.</li><li><strong>Sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratios for individual imaging modalities</strong> in TAK — not located <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"?\" title=\"لم يُعثر على رقم موثوق — no reliable data located\">?</button>.</li><li><strong>Sex ratio magnitude</strong> — F&gt;M is established; no reliable pooled ratio located <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button>.</li><li><strong>Antiplatelet therapy in TAK</strong> — no guideline recommendation retrieved; commonly used but the evidence base is weak <button class=\"وسم-دليل\" type=\"button\" data-وسم=\"L\" title=\"دليل محدود — limited evidence\">L</button>.</li><li><strong>Whether a guideline newer than the 2025 EULAR recommendations or the ACR/VF 2021 guideline has been issued.</strong> Absence cannot be verified. Check rheumatology.org/vasculitis-guideline and ard.eular.org.</li></ul><p><strong>Confirm before clinical application</strong></p><ul><li><strong>The infection-exclusion step before the first dose of immunosuppression</strong> — tuberculous, syphilitic, and bacterial aortitis and infective endocarditis. Getting this wrong is lethal, and in a TB-endemic setting with limited diagnostics the decision belongs to a senior.</li><li><strong>Every glucocorticoid dose and taper interval</strong>, for the source-chain reason above.</li><li><strong>ACE inhibitor or ARB prescribing</strong> — confirm renal artery anatomy first.</li><li><strong>Which limb&#39;s blood pressure you are treating</strong>, and whether central pressure measurement is needed.</li><li><strong>Any decision about vascular intervention</strong> — indication, timing relative to disease activity, and open versus endovascular.</li><li><strong>Whether apparent refractoriness is active disease, fixed damage, an alternative diagnosis, or occult infection.</strong></li><li><strong>Azathioprine initiation without TPMT/NUDT15 genotyping</strong>, where genotyping is unavailable.</li><li><strong>Pregnancy planning and any drug change in a woman of childbearing potential.</strong></li></ul></div>"}],"مصادر":[{"رقم":1,"نص":"Cush JJ. EULAR 2026 Recommendations on PMR, GCA, and Takayasu Arteritis. RheumNow. June 17, 2026. <a href=\"https://rheumnow.com/news/eular-2026-recommendations-pmr-gca-and-takayasu-arteritis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">rheumnow.com</a> <em>(secondary summary; author discloses AI assistance)</em>"},{"رقم":2,"نص":"2025 EULAR recommendations for the management of polymyalgia rheumatica and primary large vessel vasculitis. <em>Ann Rheum Dis</em>. 2026. <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0003496726003572\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">sciencedirect.com</a> <em>(abstract only)</em>"},{"رقم":3,"نص":"Grayson PC, Ponte C, Suppiah R, et al. 2022 American College of Rheumatology/EULAR classification criteria for Takayasu arteritis. <em>Ann Rheum Dis</em>. 2022;81(12):1654-1660. <a href=\"https://ard.eular.org/article/S0003-4967(24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ard.eular.org</a>08731-4/fulltext)"},{"رقم":4,"نص":"Misra DP, Rathore U, Patro P, et al. Validation of the 2022 American College of Rheumatology/EULAR classification criteria for Takayasu arteritis. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37018125/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>"},{"رقم":5,"نص":"Misra DP, et al. The 2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria for Takayasu arteritis predict mortality better than the 1990 ACR classification criteria. <em>Rheumatology (Oxford)</em>. 2024;63(10):e278-e280. <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/rheumatology/article/63/10/e278/7628133\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">academic.oup.com</a>"},{"رقم":6,"نص":"Takayasu Arteritis Guidelines: Guidelines Summary. Medscape. <a href=\"https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/332378-guidelines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">emedicine.medscape.com</a> <em>(secondary summary of ACR/VF 2021)</em>"},{"رقم":7,"نص":"2021 ACR/VF Guideline for the Management of Giant Cell Arteritis and Takayasu Arteritis. Vasculitis Foundation. <a href=\"https://vasculitisfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/2021-ACR-VF-Guideline-for-Management-of-Giant-Cell-Arteritis-and-Takayasu-Artheritis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">vasculitisfoundation.org</a>"},{"رقم":8,"نص":"Advances in Takayasu arteritis: An Asia Pacific perspective. <em>PMC</em>. <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9423100/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>"},{"رقم":9,"نص":"Chinese guideline for the diagnosis and treatment of Takayasu&#39;s arteritis (2023). <em>PMC</em>. <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10985707/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>"},{"رقم":10,"نص":"Taprantzis N, Chrysikos D, Shihada A, Troupis T. Prevalence and clinical severity of Takayasu arteritis angiographic types: a systematic review with meta-analysis. <em>Rheumatol Int</em>. 2025;45(10):231. <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40982119/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>"},{"رقم":11,"نص":"Management of Takayasu Arteritis — A 2026 Update. <em>Curr Rheumatol Rep</em>. 2026. <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11926-026-01214-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">link.springer.com</a> <em>(reference list only; full text not read)</em>"},{"رقم":12,"نص":"Derivation and Validation of a New Disease Activity Assessment Tool With Higher Accuracy for Takayasu Arteritis. <em>PMC</em>. <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9248800/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>"},{"رقم":13,"نص":"Outcome Measures and Biomarkers for Disease Assessment in Takayasu Arteritis. <em>Diagnostics</em>. 2022;12(10):2565. <a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-4418/12/10/2565\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">mdpi.com</a>"},{"رقم":14,"نص":"The Epidemiology and Clinical Manifestations of Takayasu Arteritis: A Descriptive Study of Case Reports. <em>PMC</em>. <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8519497/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a> <em>(43 case reports; cited only to explain why its percentages are not usable)</em>"},{"رقم":15,"نص":"Giant Cell Arteritis (GCA) &amp; Takayasu Arteritis (TAK) Guidelines. Vasculitis Foundation. <a href=\"https://vasculitisfoundation.org/giant-cell-arteritis-gca-takayasu-arteritis-tak-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">vasculitisfoundation.org</a>"}]});
